WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:05.259
Hello and welcome to this video on montages.

2
00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:11.259
Now we're going to use a key press in order to trigger a montage being played on a character.

3
00:00:11.259 --> 00:00:18.260
We're just going to use a waving animation in this instance, because it's one of the easiest ones to create.

4
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:23.260
But you can understand that this could be used for any attack or rolls,

5
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:31.059
or any other animation that you want your character to use on command.

6
00:00:31.059 --> 00:00:36.060
So the first thing that I'm going to want to do is create a new animation.

7
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:42.630
And I'm going to do this by editing an animation that already exists.

8
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:48.759
So I'm going to come in to the content drive and search for idle.

9
00:00:48.759 --> 00:00:55.549
Now you can see here we have the mm underscore idle.

10
00:00:55.549 --> 00:01:05.950
I'm just going to duplicate this and copy it into our directory.

11
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:22.060
Now I'm going to take this and I'm just going to rename it by pressing F2.

12
00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:29.459
I'm going to rename it to mm underscore waving.

13
00:01:29.459 --> 00:01:47.769
If I double click to open this up, we open up a specific interface to do with the animations.

14
00:01:47.769 --> 00:01:52.769
You can see here we have a viewport which will show us the animation at any one point.

15
00:01:52.769 --> 00:01:59.769
And below this we have a timeline that's going to show us where we are in the timeline for this animation.

16
00:01:59.769 --> 00:02:05.629
Now I've got a couple of controls and I can stop, pause and replay.

17
00:02:05.629 --> 00:02:08.629
I'm going to start off by pausing this here.

18
00:02:08.629 --> 00:02:11.629
Now I'm going to want to edit this animation.

19
00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:18.629
And in order to do that, I am going to need to be able to see the bones so I can move them around.

20
00:02:18.629 --> 00:02:25.889
So the first thing I want to do is come up to character and we have a selection for bones.

21
00:02:25.889 --> 00:02:31.889
And I'm going to change this from select only to all hierarchy.

22
00:02:31.889 --> 00:02:36.240
And now we can see the bones that we have to work with.

23
00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.650
And my goal is to create a simple waving animation.

24
00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:51.189
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to come down in and I'm going to start off with the upper arm left.

25
00:02:51.189 --> 00:02:58.020
And I'm going to rotate that up a little bit and out a little bit.

26
00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:02.620
This does not need to be perfect.

27
00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:15.939
Then I'm going to grab the lower arm and move that up to see if it looks natural.

28
00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:23.979
OK, that doesn't look terrible. Maybe move it back a little bit.

29
00:03:23.979 --> 00:03:34.599
And I might make the hand face out.

30
00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:43.680
I'm scrolling down on the mouse wheel so that I can be very accurate with the movements.

31
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:53.580
I'm going to grab the bottom of the hand and make it out a little bit.

32
00:03:53.580 --> 00:04:01.280
There you go. So I'm quite happy with the bone that I have here.

33
00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:07.280
Now, in order to save this, I'm going to want to push the plus key at the bottom.

34
00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:12.080
This will add a bone transform additive track.

35
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:20.139
See, now that we can see that we have an additive track for all of the different bones that we moved at this point.

36
00:04:20.139 --> 00:04:26.259
Now, you'll see that that is just an additive track consistent across all of these.

37
00:04:26.259 --> 00:04:30.259
I'm going to want to add a second one in in order to get this hand to move.

38
00:04:30.259 --> 00:04:33.259
So I'm moving it a little bit further in here.

39
00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:39.899
I'm going to come back down to this arm and I'm going to rotate it to the other side.

40
00:04:39.899 --> 00:04:47.459
And I'll rotate the hand slightly so that we are still in an upward motion.

41
00:04:47.459 --> 00:04:56.420
And again, I'm going to hit the plus key here.

42
00:04:56.420 --> 00:05:02.259
It creates just another track. So you can see we have a very small hand move.

43
00:05:02.259 --> 00:05:08.089
What I'll do is I'll jump back to a little bit after it and move the hand back.

44
00:05:08.089 --> 00:05:14.279
I'll move the hand back and the arm back to its nearest original position.

45
00:05:14.279 --> 00:05:18.019
Now I'm going to push the plus key again.

46
00:05:18.019 --> 00:05:25.800
So a relatively simple arm wave movement.

47
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.759
Now, this idle is actually quite a long animation.

48
00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:36.759
So in order to cut this down, I'm going to move on to around 90.

49
00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:43.019
Right hand click. I'm going to remove frame 90 to 228.

50
00:05:43.019 --> 00:05:46.529
Now we've got a far shorter animation.

51
00:05:46.529 --> 00:05:51.360
So if I just hit play, you can see we have a very, very small animation.

52
00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:57.120
I'm leaving a little bit of an in and a little bit of an out

53
00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:03.120
because we'll have a bit of a blend when we are running the montage.

54
00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.120
Again, this doesn't need to be perfect.

55
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:12.279
We just need something to work with so that we can demonstrate this.

56
00:06:12.279 --> 00:06:18.790
So I'm now going to hit save and jump back into the content browser here.

57
00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:25.560
Now we cannot directly run this animation from the Character Blueprint.

58
00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.850
In order to do this, we're going to need to use a montage.

59
00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:34.009
So I'm going to come in right hand click.

60
00:06:34.009 --> 00:06:40.970
And again, under animation, I'm going to select animation montage.

61
00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.970
I will select the SK mannequin.

62
00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:56.459
And I'm going to call this mont underscore wave.

63
00:06:56.459 --> 00:07:00.500
If I double click on this montage that I've just created,

64
00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:05.500
you can see, again, we get a very similar setup to what we previously had with the animation.

65
00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.500
Now I have an asset browser down the right hand side.

66
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.500
And I'm going to use this to find my wave.

67
00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:15.529
So if I type in WAV, see I've got my wave.

68
00:07:15.529 --> 00:07:20.529
I could drag this in and place this on the default group.

69
00:07:20.529 --> 00:07:26.939
Now, there are a number of other things that you can do with montages.

70
00:07:26.939 --> 00:07:30.939
This doesn't necessarily need to be a singular animation.

71
00:07:30.939 --> 00:07:37.129
It could actually grab another animation, for example, the full animation.

72
00:07:37.129 --> 00:07:41.129
And I could drag this in and connect the two together.

73
00:07:41.129 --> 00:07:47.509
So you can see here that these animations would play one after the other,

74
00:07:47.509 --> 00:07:49.509
not necessarily naturally.

75
00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:52.579
This gives you an example of how you might use a montage.

76
00:07:52.579 --> 00:07:57.579
You could possibly use a combination of animations to all be played together.

77
00:07:57.579 --> 00:08:05.579
And you can do things like add notifiers on this in order to trigger some logic connected to an animation.

78
00:08:06.579 --> 00:08:09.579
But in our case, we're not going to do any of that.

79
00:08:09.579 --> 00:08:16.019
We're just going to leave it with our mm underscore waving animation.

80
00:08:16.019 --> 00:08:19.019
Really short, straightforward animation.

81
00:08:19.019 --> 00:08:22.660
And I'm going to hit save.

82
00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:28.240
What's worth noting is this is playing through the default group slot.

83
00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.459
Slots can be added.

84
00:08:31.459 --> 00:08:39.779
And that's useful if you want to have different montages run through different slots in the animation.

85
00:08:39.779 --> 00:08:42.970
We'll demonstrate that in a minute.

86
00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:47.970
So let's jump back into our animation blueprint.

87
00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:53.120
And for this, I'm going to need to go back to the AmiGraph.

88
00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.120
At present, we have a very simple animation.

89
00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:00.500
All we have here is our locomotion state machine.

90
00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:03.500
Now, I'm going to add in a bit more functionality.

91
00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:10.700
I'm going to add in functionality that's going to sit between the locomotion machine and the output pose.

92
00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:16.750
So to start with, I am going to hold down the Alt key and left click.

93
00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:23.539
Now, our chart's going to get a little bit more complicated.

94
00:09:23.539 --> 00:09:29.539
And we are going to need to reference the locomotion state machine multiple times.

95
00:09:29.539 --> 00:09:33.580
At present, we cannot do that with the state machine as it is.

96
00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:38.580
What we're going to need to do is save the output as a cached pose.

97
00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:45.580
And to do this, I'm going to drag off the animation pose.

98
00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:47.580
I'm going to search for cache.

99
00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:54.480
And you can see here at the bottom, we have an option for new saved cache pose.

100
00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:02.190
And I'm just going to set the name to saved locomotion.

101
00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:11.419
So what this will allow us to do is have multiple reference to this saved locomotion.

102
00:10:11.419 --> 00:10:22.019
This is almost like a variable where we're saving out the output of the state machine.

103
00:10:22.019 --> 00:10:26.019
OK, and with that saved, I'm going to come back to the output pose.

104
00:10:26.019 --> 00:10:35.220
And I'm going to right-hand click and search for use cached.

105
00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:40.470
And that's going to allow us to have a used cached posed saved locomotion.

106
00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:43.399
I'm going to need to have this twice.

107
00:10:43.399 --> 00:10:51.399
So again, I'm going to control C copy and control V to duplicate this cached pose.

108
00:10:51.399 --> 00:11:00.580
Now, the reason we're going to want to have this twice is because we are going to split the body into two sections.

109
00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:04.580
We're going to have the upper half of the body and the lower half of the body.

110
00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:09.580
This will allow us to run animations that would only affect one half of the body.

111
00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:13.639
In this case, we're looking at the upper half.

112
00:11:13.639 --> 00:11:18.639
And this is useful. For example, you have a character that can attack with a sword.

113
00:11:18.639 --> 00:11:22.639
You don't necessarily want to take over the entire body to do it.

114
00:11:22.639 --> 00:11:26.639
You might just want to take over the top half. That's what we're doing here.

115
00:11:26.639 --> 00:11:34.500
And we're going to do that by using a layered blend per bone.

116
00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:39.659
I'm just going to search for per bone. And you can see we have a layered blend per bone.

117
00:11:39.659 --> 00:11:45.559
Now I will plug this output into the output pose.

118
00:11:45.559 --> 00:11:50.559
I'm going to plug the saved locomotion into the base pose here.

119
00:11:50.559 --> 00:12:00.539
And with this layered blend per bone selected, I'm going to come up into the details panel.

120
00:12:00.539 --> 00:12:05.539
And what I want to do is tell it which bone to split the body on.

121
00:12:05.539 --> 00:12:12.600
So under the layered setup, there is already a singular index here.

122
00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:16.600
And if I do that a drop down, you can see we have a branch filter.

123
00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:24.590
I'm going to push plus. And in this index, it's requesting a bone name.

124
00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:32.809
For this, I'm going to use spine underscore zero one.

125
00:12:32.809 --> 00:12:39.379
And if we were to jump back into our montage and onto our skeleton,

126
00:12:39.379 --> 00:12:47.330
you can see that this is right at the bottom here.

127
00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:51.419
So this is controlling the entire at the top path,

128
00:12:51.419 --> 00:12:54.419
and then the legs are controlled with the other section.

129
00:12:54.419 --> 00:12:57.419
So I've split this skeleton in two.

130
00:12:57.419 --> 00:13:07.419
So the first section, so one of the things about a layered blend per bone,

131
00:13:07.419 --> 00:13:12.419
is the first section is always going to be from root upwards.

132
00:13:12.419 --> 00:13:16.419
So this is going to control the legs.

133
00:13:16.419 --> 00:13:21.419
Now the second section, this is going to be from the rest of the skeleton.

134
00:13:21.419 --> 00:13:24.419
So this is the top half of the body.

135
00:13:24.419 --> 00:13:28.610
And I might want to play the saved locomotion.

136
00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:31.610
However, I'm going to add a slot in the middle.

137
00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:35.669
So if I come in here and I search for slot,

138
00:13:35.669 --> 00:13:39.669
you can see under montage, we have an option for slot.

139
00:13:39.669 --> 00:13:44.620
And it has default slot.

140
00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:48.620
I'm going to drag this in and connect it up to the blend pose zero.

141
00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:57.320
So this default slot is the same slot that we were running our montage in.

142
00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:00.320
So in the case that the montage is running,

143
00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:05.320
the default slot, because it comes after the locomotion,

144
00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:08.320
is going to override the locomotion.

145
00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:14.450
And this is going to send the montage data telling it to be played.

146
00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:18.450
Then it's going to be split by the layered blend per bone

147
00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.480
and given to the output pose.

148
00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:25.509
So if nothing is running through the default slot,

149
00:14:25.509 --> 00:14:28.610
we would get the saved locomotion.

150
00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:33.610
However, if we run a montage, it's going to take over this default slot here.

151
00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:40.080
So now if I save and compile, that's all working.

152
00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:44.429
But we still need to run this animation.

153
00:14:44.429 --> 00:14:49.429
So to do that, we're going to need to jump back into our third person character that we're using.

154
00:14:49.429 --> 00:14:53.460
And here I am on the event graph.

155
00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:57.460
I'm going to add in another section at the bottom here.

156
00:14:57.460 --> 00:15:00.940
I'm going to come in here, right-hand click,

157
00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:03.940
and I am going to search for KeyZ.

158
00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:09.539
You see at the bottom here we have a keyboard event for Z.

159
00:15:09.539 --> 00:15:11.539
So that gives us our keyboard event.

160
00:15:11.539 --> 00:15:15.769
And on this, we just want to play a montage.

161
00:15:15.769 --> 00:15:23.769
So I will select Play Ami Montage.

162
00:15:23.769 --> 00:15:26.769
And I need to select the Ami Montage here.

163
00:15:26.769 --> 00:15:28.769
I'm going for the wave.

164
00:15:28.769 --> 00:15:33.600
And I'm also going to increase the play rate.

165
00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:38.210
So I'm going to make that three just so it's a bit faster.

166
00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:40.210
And now if I save and compile,

167
00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:43.870
jump back in and play,

168
00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:45.870
this is working.

169
00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:48.870
You can see at the point that I press Z,

170
00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:50.870
the hand starts waving.

171
00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:55.120
And even if the character is moving,

172
00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.279
if I press Z,

173
00:15:57.279 --> 00:16:02.279
the animation just takes over the character.

174
00:16:02.279 --> 00:16:06.590
So that now brings us to the end of the exercise,

175
00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:09.590
as we can now trigger an animation

176
00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:12.820
that we've created from a montage.

177
00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:13.820
In the next video,

178
00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:17.820
we're going to have a look at the original animation blueprint

179
00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:20.820
and see how this is implemented slightly differently.

180
00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:23.169
Thank you very much.
